Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary drama; naturalistic faces, fabric, and Milwaukee/Seattle period detail from the late 1990s into the 2000s.

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A fifth-floor brick walk-up in downtown Milwaukee. Wind pushes trash along the alley. Garbage-can lids mark kickball bases.

(SOUND: distant traffic, a ball thudding off brick)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not the book. My real Big Brother. Nick.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Cramped living room. MA, older, brown hair going gray, sits near the TV in a worn chair. PETER, mid-twenties, lean, ordinary clothes, hauls grocery bags. NICK fills the doorway — tall, rough-hewn face with scars on chin and over the left eyebrow, reddish-brown hair uncombed, heavy build in a work jacket.

(SOUND: TV game-show chatter, bags rustling)

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don’t know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That’s how old people go downhill.

Peter nods, guilty. Nick sets bags down like they weigh nothing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. People moved out of the way.

EXT. MOXIE’S GYM - DAY

Corner gym. Through the glass, a man curls a forty-pound bar — then drops it the second Nick walks in. Nick nods, takes the bench, loads fifty.

(SOUND: iron plates clanging, low gym murmur)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A real-life Viking. Modern-day warrior. I lived with him and Ma after Dad took off when I was eight.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter in an apron spins dough. Through the front glass, sidewalk traffic.

(SOUND: oven hum, order ticket printer)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somebody has to be here with Ma. She’s getting older. Hard for her to see.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

SIONNA — short brown hair, pale skin, light-blue eyes, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, backpack — trips on the cement and brushes Peter’s shoulder.

(SOUND: shoe scuff, soft impact)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
That’s different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

He is not really listening — only her eyes.

PETER
Want to come in for pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives him a number, walks away, glances back over her shoulder.

(SOUND: city street bed, her footsteps fading)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Intense. Like waves at the ocean covering me, and I didn’t want to move.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma lines pill bottles on the counter, confused, glassy-eyed.

(SOUND: bottles clicking, fridge hum)

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta?

PETER
Ma, we don’t go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She stares as if she does not know him — then snaps back.

MA
Oh. Right. I am so sorry.

Peter’s face tightens.

INT. JOE’S BAR - NIGHT

Packers on the TV. Nick slaps Peter’s back, beer in hand.

(SOUND: bar crowd, game announcer, bottle clink)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

PETER
Ma was all confused today.

NICK
I knew she’d start going crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. I don’t have money for a nursing home.

Nick’s look is almost a punch. Peter pays and leaves.

EXT. CITY STREET / PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter hurries through cold air, ducks into the old library — wood floors, chain-hung lights, classic literature stacks. He checks out Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: wind, heavy door, quiet library hush)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when you could get the same thing free?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College crowd, backpacks, steam. Sionna at a back table in a loose green hooded top. Peter sits. Her eyes hold him.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low indie music)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. This guy and girl — they grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks ghosts, doubles, moors — love that never changes destroys you. Something in Peter wakes.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

PETER
Reminds me of Nick. Strong. Unwavering.

They smile. Another date — dinner Friday.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She made me feel smarter just by being around her. Maybe that was Ma’s problem. Dad left. She never moved on.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Nick stomps; Ma sits quiet in her chair. Peter in the doorway.

(SOUND: door slam echo, floorboards)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I met a girl. We had a couple dates.

NICK
A girl. That’s worth all this? Look at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out. Peter stands shaking.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Peter in T-shirt and shorts, awkward in downward dog, shirt falling over his face. Sionna beside him, easy. After class he lies in savasana — calm for the first time.

(SOUND: soft instructor voice, collective breathing)

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
I felt so dumb.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

INT. DOCTOR’S OFFICE / HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - DAY

DR. CARSON faces Peter. Ma is elsewhere in the suite.

(SOUND: clinic PA, distant siren)

DR. CARSON
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Take her to the ER.

Later: hospital room. Sionna meets Peter for lunch outside; his heart lifts. He dozes in a chair and jolts awake from a dream — Nick stabbing him.

(SOUND: monitor beeps, Peter’s sharp breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me. Only a dream. Still felt like betrayal.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter shows Ma a blue weekly pill box. Nick enters, beet-red, boots heavy, hair wild.

(SOUND: TV low, boots on floor)

NICK
Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

PETER
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

PETER
You haven’t even met her. Give her a break!

NICK
Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
You’re so goddamn hateful—

Nick slaps Peter’s head. Ear rings. Nick storms out, door slamming.

(SOUND: open-hand crack, door boom)

PETER
(whisper)
Who needs him.

EXT. JOE’S BAR - NIGHT

Later months. Inside: smoke, pool, rock. Nick eyes ex LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Rage builds. Peter steps between.

(SOUND: AC/DC on speakers, cue balls, crowd hush)

PETER
Nick, leave it. You don’t even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You’re next.

Nick’s fist draws back toward Peter’s face — slow-motion beat — then bouncers grab Nick. Chaos. Peter runs into the cold night, lungs burning, to Sionna’s door.

(SOUND: scuffle, head-butt thud, Peter’s boots pounding pavement)

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter collapses into her. She holds his face.

(SOUND: his ragged breathing, distant traffic)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares does that.

PETER
I love Nick. He’s my brother.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don’t let him stop your purpose.

She draws him down; they sleep. Dream: Sionna as angel from the lake, Nick a giant with a mace — she pushes him back with light.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Months later. Nick and Sionna finally meet over pie. Nick refuses sugar. Sionna calmly dismantles his lines about women and college with facts. Nick’s smile turns snake-thin.

(SOUND: forks on plates, tense silence)

SIONNA
Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to make a difference.

NICK
Sounds like you two have it all figured out. Doctor Sionna.

He leaves, gripping Peter’s shirt at the door.

NICK
(whisper)
Take care of Ma.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

Murphy’s Law. Rain. Peter bikes hard after finding the apartment empty, machine blinking.

(SOUND: bike chain, rain on jacket, phone beep)

ANSWERING MACHINE (V.O.)
Officer Dupuis, Milwaukee County Sheriff… Patricia Owens, Frodo Hospital… update on your family members.

INT. POLICE STATION - NIGHT

Stone building, 1898 plaque. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, faces Peter across a desk. Styrofoam cup shakes in Peter’s hands.

(SOUND: station buzz, radio crackle)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Werner and Lowe. Your brother Nicholas is in serious condition. Your mother, Olivia Olson, passed away at the scene.

Peter’s face empties.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology pending. We think he may have been drunk.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - NIGHT

Through glass: Nick’s wild hair, tubes, chest drain, broken leg. Peter will not go in.

(SOUND: monitors, soft shoes on tile)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He fucking killed my mom.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Empty chair. Afghan. Peter breaks — fists in hair, sobbing, screaming at the walls.

(SOUND: raw crying, fist on wall)

PETER
Die you jackass. Die.

Sionna arrives, holds him.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We’ll figure it out.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket. Few mourners. Dirt hits the lid. Later at the grave: Olivia Anne Olsen, 1933–1998. Peter sets mums.

(SOUND: muted thunk of dirt, wind in trees)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I have fought the good fight. You did, Ma.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Days later. Nick yellow-bruised, oxygen, weak. Tears.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, hallway cart)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Can’t believe Ma’s gone. Three Jack and Cokes… then church. Vehicular manslaughter. Mistake.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick flares; tubes and cast hold him powerless. Peter turns and walks out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits about Nick.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Gray day outside. Nick forces his way in — thinner, raging, switchblade. Sionna backs to the wall. He grips her throat, blade at collarbone.

(SOUND: door crack, her choked breath, knife snick)

NICK
You’re the reason I’m going to jail. Break it off. Cash in a week. Or this blade.

Front door bangs. Peter lunges. Struggle. Blade bites — blood. Nick elbows Peter, wipes the knife on her shirt, flees.

(SOUND: body impact, wet gasp, running feet)

PETER
Holy shit— Sionna—

He clamps the wound, dials 911, blood on his hands.

(SOUND: his shouting into phone, approaching sirens)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If I hadn’t walked in, she would have died in that hallway.

INT. SIONNA’S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months later. Sionna in a chair, blank ocean eyes, shoulder stiff from surgery. Peter kneels.

(SOUND: clock tick, winter quiet)

SIONNA
I can’t feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. Maybe a break. Mom and Dad’s.

PETER
Stay. We can work it out.

She walks to the bedroom. He leaves into the void.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was my life guide. Then free fall.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET / BELLTOWN - DAY

Years later. Gray blanket sky, mist, hoods. PETER, older, fuller shoulders, walks past a keyboard player. Signage: TONY’S BOY’S PIZZA under a medical building.

(SOUND: light rain, ferry horn distant, keyboard riff)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years. My own chain. Tony’s name in honor. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. LOWELL’S / PIKE PLACE - DAY

Peter at a window table, mountains hidden in cloud. Cinnamon roll like a halo. Memory hits — blood, sirens, Sionna pale.

(SOUND: market murmur, cup set down)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Outer flesh heals. Inner wounds linger. She finally blamed me. I finished the degree. Built the business. Still the void.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles, citrus-lavender. Theme: gratefulness. Peter on a mat. After, home to cat BOZO.

(SOUND: soft chant, mat unroll)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Shakespeare — nothing either good or bad but thinking makes it so. I had to find forgiveness.

He dreams a sage on a green road; a dirty pizza box; judgment dropped. Wakes smiling, writes it down.

INT. COFFEE SHOP - DAY

Blonde barista CHRIS, hoop earrings, apron. Peter asks her out to a play. Sun breaks. He books a flight to Milwaukee on his laptop.

(SOUND: grinder, register bell)

PETER
I have to see Nick. Put this behind me forever.

EXT. OLD KIRK STREET / FORMER PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter in a rental car. Building unchanged; alley dumpsters. Parlor is now a cosmetology school. He meets BOB in a deli — older, thicker — bear hug.

(SOUND: street noise, deli chatter)

BOB
You got out and did something. Good for you, man.

PETER
I need to let the shit go.

EXT. SIONNA’S OLD DUPLEX - DAY

Grayer paint, naked porch. Peter sits in the car, remembers bamboo rockers, the cat, I love you. Starts the engine.

(SOUND: idle engine, kids on skateboards)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I love her. I want her happy. Leave her alone. Move forward.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - VISITING ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass, phones. NICK enters — older, graying, scrawny, sad watery eyes. They pick up receivers.

(SOUND: locks clanging, muffled voices, phone plastic)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
I’m here to tell you some things. Just listen.

Beat.

PETER
You’re an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

Nick’s eyes shine.

PETER
And this is the most important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. Releases your control forever.

Tears run down Nick’s face onto the Formica.

NICK
You’re so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. You’re more than I ever was.

GUARD (O.S.)
Time’s almost up.

PETER
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
I love you too. What are you doing now?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn’t. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. I’m so proud of you.

Guards take Nick. Peter hangs up, light.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT - DAY

Rolling green hills. Peter drives, then floats through the terminal. Boarding scan.

(SOUND: tires on wet road, airport PA, scanner beep)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Weight of thirty years — gone. Somewhere she’s happy. I send Nick love and know I will never see him again by choice.

He whispers at the gate, almost smiling.

PETER
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
